{"tool_name":"swiss-model_workspace","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"swiss-model_workspace","home":"https://swissmodel.expasy.org/","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0","summary":"SWISS-MODEL is a fully automated protein structure homology-modelling server, accessible via the ExPASy web server, or from the program DeepView (Swiss Pdb-Viewer). The purpose of this server is to make protein modelling accessible to all life science researchers worldwide.","addition_date":"2016-06-22T07:26:39Z","last_update_date":"2020-07-06T09:37:08Z","tool_type":["Web application"],"collections":["3D-BioInfo","3D-BioInfo-Protein-Complex","3D-BioInfo-Protein-Ligand-Interactions","3D-BioInfo-Structure-Function","COVID-19"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:swiss-model_workspace","credit":[{"email":"torsten.schwede@unibas.ch","name":"Torsten Schwede","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2715-335X","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"],"url":"http://schwedelab.org"},{"email":"help-swissmodel@unibas.ch","typeEntity":"Project","typeRole":["Support"]},{"name":"SIB Swiss Institute of Bioinformatics","typeEntity":"Institute","typeRole":["Provider"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"https://swissmodel.expasy.org/docs/help"}],"edit_permission":{"authors":["SMNG","gerardo.tauriello"],"type":"group"},"function":[{"input":[{"data":{"term":"Protein sequence (raw)","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_2974"}},{"data":{"term":"UniProt ID","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_2291"}},{"data":{"term":"UniProt accession","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_3021"}},{"data":{"term":"Protein sequence (raw)","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_2974"},"format":[{"term":"FASTA","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_1929"}]},{"data":{"term":"Sequence alignment (protein)","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_1384"},"format":[{"term":"FASTA","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_1929"}]}],"note":"Template structures","operation":[{"term":"Protein modelling","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0477"}],"output":[{"data":{"term":"Protein structure","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_1460"},"format":[{"term":"PDB","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_1476"},{"term":"mmCIF","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_1477"}]}]}],"name":"SWISS-MODEL Workspace","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"Torsten.Schwede","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/nar/gky427","metadata":{"abstract":"\u00a9 The Author(s) 2018. Published by Oxford University Press on behalf of Nucleic Acids Research.Homology modelling has matured into an important technique in structural biology, significantly contributing to narrowing the gap between known protein sequences and experimentally determined structures. Fully automated workflows and servers simplify and streamline the homology modelling process, also allowing users without a specific computational expertise to generate reliable protein models and have easy access to modelling results, their visualization and interpretation. Here, we present an update to the SWISS-MODEL server, which pioneered the field of automated modelling 25 years ago and been continuously further developed. Recently, its functionality has been extended to the modelling of homo- and heteromeric complexes. Starting from the amino acid sequences of the interacting proteins, both the stoichiometry and the overall structure of the complex are inferred by homology modelling. Other major improvements include the implementation of a new modelling engine, ProMod3 and the introduction a new local model quality estimation method, QMEANDisCo. SWISS-MODEL is freely available at https://swissmodel.expasy.org.","authors":[{"name":"Bertoni M."},{"name":"Bienert S."},{"name":"Bordoli L."},{"name":"De Beer T.A.P."},{"name":"Gumienny R."},{"name":"Heer F.T."},{"name":"Lepore R."},{"name":"Rempfer C."},{"name":"Schwede T."},{"name":"Studer G."},{"name":"Tauriello G."},{"name":"Waterhouse A."}],"citationCount":2837,"date":"2018-07-02T